# Rust build artifacts. The yidam template repository this was derived from carries a
# 6.1 GB crates/*/target tree; keeping it out of history matters here.
target/
**/target/

# macOS
.DS_Store

# Downloaded source publications. Refetch with `edfund-connect fetch --all`; the SHA-256 of
# each is pinned in crates/connect/source-digests.txt, so what was used stays known.
.cache/

# Wrangler's local scratch, written into the repository root by any `pages deploy`. It holds no
# credentials — those live in ~/Library/Preferences/.wrangler — but it does hold the account id
# and the account's display name, and it is machine state rather than anything about the model.
.wrangler/

# Astro writes generated types beside whatever directory it is invoked from. `web/.gitignore`
# covers the intended location; this covers an invocation from the repository root.
.astro/

# The yidam CLI, installed per-repository rather than per-machine. `mise run
# yidam-build` puts it at .yidam/bin/ beside the pin in .yidam.toml, so the binary
# and the vendored prelude always agree — one ~/.cargo/bin is last-writer-wins
# across every yidam repository on the machine, and the loser runs a binary that
# does not match its own prelude with nothing anywhere saying so.
#
# A binary is build output and does not belong in history. The two metadata files
# are cargo's install ledger for that root, written beside the bin/ directory.
.yidam/bin/
.yidam/.crates.toml
.yidam/.crates2.json

# `yidam embed` composes each node's and each catalog entry's prose into one text file per
# node, as input to `yidam index-build`. Derived output, and derived from something already
# committed: every character in it is a copy of the corpus or the catalog, so it is the
# corpus twice in history, rewritten in bulk by anything that changes how prose is composed.
# Declaring the four prose fields moved it from 462K characters to 746K across 174 files in
# one commit's worth of work.
#
# Nothing reads it. There is no vector index here — `index-build` needs the `index` feature,
# which is not in the released binary's default set — and `.yidam/config.toml` omits
# `[due] index_after` deliberately so no clock reports a debt nobody intends to pay. It is
# regenerated with `mise run embed` on the day an index is decided on, which is the same
# argument `.yidam/bin/` above is ignored under: build output, cheap to remake, obsolete the
# moment the thing it was built from moves.
.yidam/embeddings/
